# .NET build output
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/
[Dd]ebug/
[Rr]elease/
*.user
*.suo
*.userosscache
*.sln.docstates
.vs/
TestResults/
artifacts/

# Frontend build output and dependencies
node_modules/
src/Frontend/dist/
npm-debug.log*

# Local logs
*.log

# The debugging profile passes JID and password on the command line - a
# working account in plain text, one 'git add' away from the history.
**/Properties/launchSettings.json

# The XMPP account of this web app: JID, password and endpoint, written by
# the account page. A working account in plain text, so never part of the
# repository - the same rule as for launchSettings.json above.
/xmpp-account.json

# The login of the web page itself, written by the settings page. Only a
# password hash, but still nobody else's business.
/web-login.json

# The chat archive: everything that was ever said, plus the files that were
# shared. Written by the app, one directory per account and conversation.
# The most private thing this program produces - never part of the repository.
/chats/

# A security review of this repository: which weaknesses are open, in which
# file, on which line, and what an attacker would do with each. Worth having
# beside the code and exactly wrong to publish with it - committing it here
# would put the list of ways in on GitHub, next to the code it describes and
# ahead of the fixes.
/SECURITY-REPORT.md
